[[Balisca]] is a {{W|Continental fragment|continental fragment}} east of {{W|South America}} in the {{W|South Atlantic Ocean}} (which it is completely surrounded by) and the {{W|Drake Passage|Mar de Hoces}} to the south. Balisca is long and narrow—over {{convert|1600|km|mi}} along its north-north-east axis with a maximum width of {{convert|700|km|mi}}—with about {{convert|15000|km|mi|-2|abbr=on}} of coastline and a total land area of {{convert|1526052|km2}}, making it the {{W|List of countries and dependencies by area|world’s 28th-largest country}}. Because of its far-flung outlying islands and long coastline, the country has extensive marine resources. Its [[W:Exclusive economic zone of Balisca|exclusive economic zone]] is one of the largest in the world, covering more than 3 times its land area. The country, including all of the islands it controls, lies between the {{W|Tropic of Capricorn}} and the {{W|Antarctic Circle}}. The main islands, from north to south, are [[Pindorama]], [[Sicania]], [[Atlántida]] and [[Tinguiririca]]. Together they are often known as the [[Baliscan archipelago]]. It is the largest island country in the Americas, and the second largest in the world, after {{W|Indonesia}}.

The country owes its varied topography, and perhaps even its emergence above the waves, to the dynamic boundary it straddles between the [[W:South American Plate|South American]], [[Hesperian Plate|Hesperian]], {{W|Scotia Plate|Scotia}}, and the {{W|Sandwich Plate|Sandwich}} and [[Siculian Plate|Siculian]] plates. Balisca is part of the [[Hesperian Microcontinent]], a {{W|microcontinent}} nearly half the size of Brazil that gradually submerged after breaking away from the [[Gondwana|Gondwanan]] supercontinent. About 25 million years ago, a shift in {{W|plate tectonic}} movements began to [[W:Kaikoura orogeny|contort and crumple]] the region. This is now most evident in the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], formed by [[W:Continental collision|compression of the crust]] beside the [[? Fault]]. Elsewhere, the plate boundary involves the {{W|subduction}} of one plate under the other, producing the [[Augusta Trench]] to the north, the [[Concordian Trench]] east of the [[Araucaria]], and the [[Arcadian Trench|Arcadian]] and [[Lucanian Trench|Lucanian Trenches]] further north.

Atlántida is the largest landmass of Balisca. It is divided along its length by the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. There are 18 peaks over {{convert|3000|m|ft}}, the highest of which is is [[Tanimbuka]] (‘ash mountain’) in the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén 4,822.6 metres (15,822 ft) above sea level). The [[Strait of Ivorra|Strait of Ivorra's]] steep mountains and deep [[W:Fjord|fiords]] record the extensive ice age glaciation of this southwestern corner of the [[Cerdanya]]'s [[Tinguiririca]]. The island of [[Pindorama]] is less mountainous but is [[Volcanology of Balisca|marked by volcanism]]. The highly active [[Peke'i Volcanic Zone]] has formed a large [[Peke'i Volcanic Plateau|volcanic plateau]], punctuated by the island's highest mountain, [[Ybyrá]] ({{convert|3621|m}}). The plateau also hosts the Pindorama's largest lake, [[Lac del Cràter]], nestled in the {{W|caldera}} of one of the world's most active [[W:Supervolcano|supervolcanoes]]. Balisca is prone to [[List of earthquakes in Balisca|earthquakes]] and to a lesser extent, [[Volcanism of Balisca|volcanic]] eruptions.

